WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:14.389 --> 00:00:18.601
Well, when I started my graduate work

00:00:18.602 --> 00:00:23.689
in genetics the big question was

00:00:23.690 --> 00:00:25.483
what on earth is this gene?

00:00:25.484 --> 00:00:27.860
How can it do these amazing things

00:00:27.861 --> 00:00:31.197
that it does? And it was a complete mystery.

00:00:31.198 --> 00:00:34.492
That was the big question, that was the question that

00:00:34.493 --> 00:00:38.246
attracted me to genetics and that made me want to spend my life

00:00:38.247 --> 00:00:40.581
trying to get an answer or get part of the

00:00:40.582 --> 00:00:43.417
answer. By that time, a lot was known about

00:00:43.418 --> 00:00:47.505
how genes are transmitted in heredity

00:00:47.506 --> 00:00:50.925
and some of the things that they control.

00:00:50.926 --> 00:00:54.554
But nothing was known about the physical

00:00:54.555 --> 00:00:57.640
chemical structure that was responsible for

00:00:57.641 --> 00:01:01.310
what the gene could do. And it seemed very inaccessible

00:01:01.311 --> 00:01:08.276
and very hard to get at. And that was the question

00:01:08.277 --> 00:01:10.778
that was mostly on my mind. To find a way to work

00:01:10.779 --> 00:01:13.656
that would help to decide what the gene

00:01:13.657 --> 00:01:16.951
was like and how it could do what it was doing.

00:01:16.952 --> 00:01:20.788
I was working with bacteria. Bacteria

00:01:20.789 --> 00:01:23.249
had become a very good material to work with

00:01:23.250 --> 00:01:28.546
in genetics, because in 1943 while I was a

00:01:28.547 --> 00:01:32.884
graduate student, for the first time, it became clear

00:01:32.885 --> 00:01:35.761
that bacteria had genes like other organisms.

00:01:35.762 --> 00:01:40.099
Before that, the bacteriologists were telling us that

00:01:40.100 --> 00:01:42.435
bacteria just change with the environment.

00:01:42.436 --> 00:01:45.062
When it was established that they had

00:01:45.063 --> 00:01:47.857
regular genes and they could be used for genetic

00:01:47.858 --> 00:01:51.444
experiments, that's when I decided I wanted to work with

00:01:51.445 --> 00:01:55.031
bacteria. I was just beginning the research for my PhD

00:01:55.032 --> 00:01:59.243
and I had planned to do it with the fruit fly,

00:01:59.244 --> 00:02:01.871
which was the standard organism in genetics

00:02:01.872 --> 00:02:03.497
and a wonderful little bug.

00:02:03.498 --> 00:02:06.125
And I was going to try to understand how

00:02:06.126 --> 00:02:11.464
radiation induces mutation in fruit fly, Drosophila.

00:02:11.465 --> 00:02:15.968
When I read that paper proving that bacteria

00:02:15.969 --> 00:02:19.805
had genes, I really got excited and thought

00:02:19.806 --> 00:02:22.016
that would be a wonderful material to work with.

00:02:22.017 --> 00:02:26.270
I mean, they multiply every 20 minutes and you could

00:02:26.271 --> 00:02:28.356
hold a billion of them in a little test tube.

00:02:28.357 --> 00:02:31.025
And so just think how many generations

00:02:31.026 --> 00:02:33.819
you could work with in very short time.

00:02:33.820 --> 00:02:37.823
So, that's when my advisor at Columbia,

00:02:37.824 --> 00:02:43.913
Professor Dobzhansky, saw how excited I was about

00:02:43.914 --> 00:02:46.999
this new development using bacteria for genetics.

00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:50.002
He suggested that I might want to do my PhD

00:02:50.003 --> 00:02:52.922
research with E. coli instead of Drosophila.

00:02:52.923 --> 00:02:56.843
And that's why he sent me to Cold Spring Harbor

00:02:56.844 --> 00:03:00.596
for the summer. To learn how to work with bacteria.

00:03:00.597 --> 00:03:04.517
Luria and Delbruck the ones who had discovered that

00:03:04.518 --> 00:03:06.143
bacteria had genes and they were going to be

00:03:06.144 --> 00:03:08.020
there in Cold Spring Harbor in that summer.

00:03:08.021 --> 00:03:14.652
And so I did go and that was one of the

00:03:14.653 --> 00:03:18.990
most fortunate events in my working life

00:03:18.991 --> 00:03:21.868
that I've connected with Cold Spring Harbor.

00:03:21.869 --> 00:03:25.204
Because it turns out, I actually stayed there for

00:03:25.205 --> 00:03:29.208
11 years. I had a kind of beginner's luck,

00:03:29.209 --> 00:03:32.003
I think. The very first experiment I did, I had

00:03:32.004 --> 00:03:36.841
no idea what I was doing. The director at

00:03:36.842 --> 00:03:39.051
the laboratory at Cold Spring Harbor handed me

00:03:39.052 --> 00:03:43.556
a culture of E. coli in a test tube and he pointed

00:03:43.557 --> 00:03:48.561
to an ultraviolet lamp, and said "Go. Induce mutations."

00:03:48.562 --> 00:03:53.524
So I had to sort of feel my way as to how to work

00:03:53.525 --> 00:03:56.110
with these bugs. I watched other people and saw

00:03:56.111 --> 00:03:59.447
how they worked. And I started my very first experiment

00:03:59.448 --> 00:04:03.910
and the first thing I needed to do was to decide

00:04:03.911 --> 00:04:07.413
what does of ultraviolet light to use, because there were no

00:04:07.414 --> 00:04:12.710
published information about that. So I was stabbing

00:04:12.711 --> 00:04:16.130
in the dark at the first try. And in my first try

00:04:16.131 --> 00:04:19.800
I overdid the dose, I used doses much too high

00:04:19.801 --> 00:04:23.221
and the next day, when I looked at my

00:04:23.222 --> 00:04:27.183
petri dishes, I found that I had put a million bacteria

00:04:27.184 --> 00:04:30.853
on the surface of each of them. And the next day,

00:04:30.854 --> 00:04:33.940
nobody grew. There was nothing on any of the plates.

00:04:33.941 --> 00:04:38.569
Except one petri dish had four colonies

00:04:38.570 --> 00:04:41.697
growing on it. So four bacteria out of a million

00:04:41.698 --> 00:04:44.283
that I had put on that petri dish had somehow

00:04:44.284 --> 00:04:49.247
survived this very high dose. And it occurred to me

00:04:49.248 --> 00:04:54.752
that maybe those four were mutants resistant to radiation

00:04:54.753 --> 00:04:59.340
because in Cold Spring Harbor at that point, there was

00:04:59.341 --> 00:05:05.012
a lot of talk about resistance. Dr. Demerec was working on

00:05:05.013 --> 00:05:07.849
resistance to penicillin, which had just been discovered.

00:05:07.850 --> 00:05:11.477
And there was resistance to this, that, and the other that people

00:05:11.478 --> 00:05:15.690
were working on. So I thought, why did they survive?

00:05:15.691 --> 00:05:17.775
Just four bacteria out of millions?

00:05:17.776 --> 00:05:20.695
And I cultured them and tested them

00:05:20.696 --> 00:05:24.031
and sure enough, they could withstand at least 100x

00:05:24.032 --> 00:05:28.661
more radiation, both ultraviolet light and later we found

00:05:28.662 --> 00:05:32.790
x-rays, than the parent strain. It turned out

00:05:32.791 --> 00:05:38.212
to be a clue to a whole new area that I was just starting

00:05:38.213 --> 00:05:43.342
to work on. And it led me to understanding that ultraviolet

00:05:43.343 --> 00:05:46.929
light does a lot more than just induce mutations.

00:05:46.930 --> 00:05:55.813
It activates what we now know to be 43 genes, at least

00:05:55.814 --> 00:05:59.692
43 different genes in E. coli that are quiet and

00:05:59.693 --> 00:06:04.155
silent when the cell is healthy, but are suddenly

00:06:04.156 --> 00:06:09.785
activated and start turning out their product when the

00:06:09.786 --> 00:06:14.415
DNA is damaged by radiation or some other agents.

00:06:14.416 --> 00:06:18.794
My first mutation sort of led me into

00:06:18.795 --> 00:06:21.672
perceiving that this was going on.

00:06:21.673 --> 00:06:25.593
And it was a discovery of what we call the SOS response

00:06:25.594 --> 00:06:31.474
in E. coli, which implies that it's a life saving mechanism

00:06:31.475 --> 00:06:36.187
which it is indeed. Because of the activities that are turned on

00:06:36.188 --> 00:06:40.691
by these genes that are activated by the damage

00:06:40.692 --> 00:06:45.947
promote the repair of the damage and the survival

00:06:45.948 --> 00:06:48.491
of the cell in a variety of different ways.

00:06:48.492 --> 00:06:51.994
And the survival of the population, actually.

00:06:51.995 --> 00:06:55.248
And I think that my experience in Cold Spring Harbor

00:06:55.249 --> 00:07:00.878
was what sort of softened me up to think in terms of repair

00:07:00.879 --> 00:07:04.465
because one of the people I met at Cold Spring Harbor was

00:07:04.466 --> 00:07:08.469
a scientist named Barbara McClintock, who won the Nobel Prize

00:07:08.470 --> 00:07:13.182
in her 80s, I think. For the amazing work that she did

00:07:13.183 --> 00:07:17.395
in genetics. It was a pleasure to get to know her and

00:07:17.396 --> 00:07:20.481
one of the things that I remember best about being

00:07:20.482 --> 00:07:25.027
at Cold Spring Harbor was the many times that I spent

00:07:25.028 --> 00:07:28.531
in Barbara McClintock's laboratory, looking over her shoulder

00:07:28.532 --> 00:07:31.868
and watching her work. When she was making her major

00:07:31.869 --> 00:07:36.372
discovery, when she found that genes actually can

00:07:36.373 --> 00:07:39.709
move from one location to another, this was very

00:07:39.710 --> 00:07:43.588
revolutionary. We always thought that a chromosome

00:07:43.589 --> 00:07:46.632
was very stable and genes stay put, but

00:07:46.633 --> 00:07:50.094
she showed that they can jump and they can have

00:07:50.095 --> 00:07:55.141
important effects when they do. At any rate, one of the things

00:07:55.142 --> 00:08:00.438
that Barbara was working on, and she talked about this

00:08:00.439 --> 00:08:06.194
at our meetings at Cold Spring Harbor, she was studying

00:08:06.195 --> 00:08:09.405
how chromosomes join together when they're broken.

00:08:09.406 --> 00:08:13.784
Because chromosomes can break as a result of

00:08:13.785 --> 00:08:18.998
various treatments, and when they do, she noticed

00:08:18.999 --> 00:08:22.001
that they come together very rapidly.

00:08:22.002 --> 00:08:28.508
The broken ends join and she was very puzzled about

00:08:28.509 --> 00:08:33.471
how that could work, but she did make a point of saying

00:08:33.472 --> 00:08:36.599
that they could -- the broken ends could find each

00:08:36.600 --> 00:08:39.602
other over a long distance through the very different

00:08:39.603 --> 00:08:43.856
parts of the nucleus, they would sort of aim at each other.

00:08:43.857 --> 00:08:46.025
There seemed to be a force that was bringing them together.

00:08:46.026 --> 00:08:50.238
And since genes are in chromosomes

00:08:50.239 --> 00:08:54.450
and chromosomes are being repaired

00:08:54.451 --> 00:08:57.662
when they're broken, it seemed to me that there

00:08:57.663 --> 00:09:01.374
must be some kind of molecular repair going on

00:09:01.375 --> 00:09:04.627
inside the chromosome that would involve carrying the gene.

00:09:04.628 --> 00:09:08.840
So I think that exposure to the way broken chromosomes

00:09:08.841 --> 00:09:13.636
are repaired made me think in terms of repair when I

00:09:13.637 --> 00:09:17.849
was considering what was happening in irradiated bacteria.

00:09:17.850 --> 00:09:22.311
Before the DNA is damaged, let's say by radiation,

00:09:22.312 --> 00:09:27.191
the DNA is quietly replicating itself, copying itself.

00:09:27.192 --> 00:09:32.947
And when DNA is damaged by ultraviolet light,

00:09:32.948 --> 00:09:35.449
for instance, which changes one of the elements

00:09:35.450 --> 00:09:41.205
of the DNA and stops the replication, the enzymes that are

00:09:41.206 --> 00:09:45.126
copying the DNA cannot get by that damaged spot.

00:09:45.127 --> 00:09:49.881
So the whole thing stops. And that's lethal if nothing happens.

00:09:49.882 --> 00:09:55.219
Because if the replication is stopped and there's nothing

00:09:55.220 --> 00:09:58.764
that can restart it, that's the end of that cell.

00:09:58.765 --> 00:10:05.229
But we learned later that E. coli and all cells

00:10:05.230 --> 00:10:08.900
including human cells, have developed repair mechanisms

00:10:08.901 --> 00:10:14.739
that can in various ways, take care of that damage and

00:10:14.740 --> 00:10:17.033
get rid of it, one way or another. There are a number

00:10:17.034 --> 00:10:21.787
of different ways to do it. And usually, they work very efficiently

00:10:21.788 --> 00:10:24.373
and the damage just goes away and the replication

00:10:24.374 --> 00:10:29.378
continues. And that happens, as I say, in a number of

00:10:29.379 --> 00:10:33.508
ways. For instance, if you shine visible light on the

00:10:33.509 --> 00:10:36.385
E. coli after it's been damaged by ultraviolet

00:10:36.386 --> 00:10:40.223
that will take care of getting rid of the damage. The visible

00:10:40.224 --> 00:10:44.310
light splits that lump that's sitting on the DNA

00:10:44.311 --> 00:10:47.980
and makes it go away. So that's an immediate repair.

00:10:47.981 --> 00:10:50.775
And then there's another repair mechanism

00:10:50.776 --> 00:10:53.319
that's called the "cut and patch" mechanism.

00:10:53.320 --> 00:10:59.325
Actually, excision repair. Which detects the damaged

00:10:59.326 --> 00:11:03.621
spot, cuts one strand of the DNA on one side of the

00:11:03.622 --> 00:11:07.917
damage, cuts on the other side, and thereby removes

00:11:07.918 --> 00:11:11.587
a small piece of the DNA containing the damage.

00:11:11.588 --> 00:11:15.508
And that leaves a gap, see, DNA has two strands,

00:11:15.509 --> 00:11:20.638
so there's one strand that's still in tact and the other one has

00:11:20.639 --> 00:11:23.307
a piece taken out of it by this cut and patch mechanism.

00:11:23.308 --> 00:11:27.520
And another enzyme comes along and fills in

00:11:27.521 --> 00:11:31.858
that patch, using the information on the other strand

00:11:31.859 --> 00:11:35.862
to do it accurately. So that's another repair mechanism

00:11:35.863 --> 00:11:38.781
that acts very quickly and that takes care of most of the

00:11:38.782 --> 00:11:42.827
damage that happens. But sometimes, there's a kind of damage

00:11:42.828 --> 00:11:46.747
that cannot be easily repaired by these mechanisms.

00:11:46.748 --> 00:11:50.626
And that's when the SOS response happens.

00:11:50.627 --> 00:11:53.462
So here you have damage sitting on the DNA

00:11:53.463 --> 00:11:56.883
which the visible light couldn't help and the cut and patch

00:11:56.884 --> 00:12:01.012
couldn't help for some reason, and then what happens is

00:12:01.013 --> 00:12:05.600
the stalled replication, the place where the replication

00:12:05.601 --> 00:12:11.647
has stopped, generates a signal that says "SOS!"

00:12:11.648 --> 00:12:19.864
Basically. And then a number of things happen,

00:12:19.865 --> 00:12:21.866
and I can't really describe them in great detail,

00:12:21.867 --> 00:12:27.079
but the two strands of DNA come apart into a sort of

00:12:27.080 --> 00:12:31.584
bubble and that's the signal for the SOS system

00:12:31.585 --> 00:12:34.212
is single stranded DNA, which normally doesn't

00:12:34.213 --> 00:12:37.882
occur in the happily replicating DNA. But the single strands

00:12:37.883 --> 00:12:42.970
bubbling out cause another molecule to come and bind

00:12:42.971 --> 00:12:47.892
those single strands. And that sets off a sequence

00:12:47.893 --> 00:12:53.397
which leads to turning on 43 genes. You know,

00:12:53.398 --> 00:12:55.233
there were a lot of people involved in that, and I haven't

00:12:55.234 --> 00:12:57.902
mentioned all the people, but the SOS response was

00:12:57.903 --> 00:13:02.907
a joint work. And this was a young man,

00:13:02.908 --> 00:13:06.160
whose name was Miroslav Radman, he was a

00:13:06.161 --> 00:13:11.541
postdoctoral fellow at Harvard and I had written a paper

00:13:11.542 --> 00:13:18.089
in which I suggested that ultraviolet light was inducing

00:13:18.090 --> 00:13:25.555
two different consequences in a similar way that seemed

00:13:25.556 --> 00:13:32.061
as if they were being induced together. And Radman

00:13:32.062 --> 00:13:38.568
a few years later, had linked to other ultraviolet effects

00:13:38.569 --> 00:13:41.863
and suggested that they were both induced by the same

00:13:41.864 --> 00:13:47.368
signal. And one of them was the same one that I had

00:13:47.369 --> 00:13:52.373
linked, so we both linked A, I had linked it to B,

00:13:52.374 --> 00:13:58.296
and he had linked it to C. And when we met, I guess it was

00:13:58.297 --> 00:14:05.386
in 1972, he had just described his end of it. And

00:14:05.387 --> 00:14:09.348
we met and we put together this observation that

00:14:09.349 --> 00:14:13.436
I had linked two events that were caused by UV

00:14:13.437 --> 00:14:16.189
and said that they were induced together, and he had

00:14:16.190 --> 00:14:19.775
linked two others, and one of them was the common one.

00:14:19.776 --> 00:14:23.529
So there we had three different ultraviolet

00:14:23.530 --> 00:14:26.824
effects that we thought were induced in a common

00:14:26.825 --> 00:14:32.622
way by a single signal. And that was really the beginning,

00:14:32.623 --> 00:14:34.707
and by the way, Radman was the one who introduced

00:14:34.708 --> 00:14:39.420
the term "SOS." And it was used in the sense of

00:14:39.421 --> 00:14:43.758
the international distress signal. So in a way, our

00:14:43.759 --> 00:14:46.219
putting together our two separate pieces of this

00:14:46.220 --> 00:14:50.348
began the SOS response to be appreciated.

00:14:50.349 --> 00:14:54.769
It was a great joy to be a part of the group that

00:14:54.770 --> 00:14:57.522
was working on DNA repair. I mean, it was a small

00:14:57.523 --> 00:15:00.441
international group, there must have been probably

00:15:00.442 --> 00:15:05.947
I'd say 30-35 individual scientists and the group of

00:15:05.948 --> 00:15:08.950
students around them and so on, who were trying

00:15:08.951 --> 00:15:12.954
to understand how the DNA changes and how it's repaired

00:15:12.955 --> 00:15:17.917
and all of that. And being part of that group was

00:15:17.918 --> 00:15:20.753
one of the most joyful experiences I ever had

00:15:20.754 --> 00:15:24.173
in science, because it was a group of people with a

00:15:24.174 --> 00:15:28.219
very common set of goals and I sometimes hear

00:15:28.220 --> 00:15:30.221
these days that there's a lot of competitiveness

00:15:30.222 --> 00:15:33.516
in science. It doesn't ring a bell with the way it was

00:15:33.517 --> 00:15:37.895
in my experience, at all. We shared our information,

00:15:37.896 --> 00:15:41.232
we shared our strains, we cheered for each other

00:15:41.233 --> 00:15:44.944
when somebody made an important discovery, and

00:15:44.945 --> 00:15:47.989
it was a human experience that was extremely

00:15:47.990 --> 00:15:53.953
satisfying. And when I look back at my work, that's a big part

00:15:53.954 --> 00:15:58.542
of why I'm glad I did science.

